[c0006] **What landed** — all in `src/editor/mod.rs`:
[c0006] - `cell_under_ray(ray, cols, rows, gap)` — pure: `Ray3d::intersect_plane` against the y=0 ground plane, then `c0002`'s `world_to_cell`, then the row bound. `world_to_cell` is open upwards by design, so rejecting a hit past the last row is this function's job.
[c0006] - `editor_pick_cell` — the plumbing: primary window's `cursor_position` → the camera marked `EditorCamera` → `viewport_to_world` → `HoveredCell`. Every step is `Option`; no hovered cell is a normal answer.
[c0006] - `HoveredCell` resource, written only when the cell changes (so change detection means "entered a different cell" — the signal `c0007` wants), cleared on the way out of the editor.
[c0006] - A gizmo outline of the block footprint on the hovered cell.
[c0006] **On the tilted-camera criterion:** it predates `c0005`'s decision to make the editor camera orthographic and straight down, so it can't be about the camera the editor actually uses. I read it as the constraint it still is — the maths must not assume a viewpoint — and satisfied it two ways: a pure test feeding rays from the exact position `setup_3d_environment` computes from `TILTED_CAMERA`/`CAMERA_TILT`, and an end-to-end test that swaps that perspective camera into a running editor and round-trips every cell of a 9×6 grid through a real viewport. Both the reading and the reasoning are written on the card.
